CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Isha adds one drop of blue ink to each of three equal beakers of water at 5, 25 and 65 degrees Celsius without stirring. After one minute the colour has spread through the hottest beaker, partly through the middle beaker and only a little in the coldest beaker. "
   "(i) Name the process of spreading of ink on its own. (ii) In which beaker was it fastest? (iii) Which property of particles explains the spreading? (iv) Why is the spreading faster at higher temperature?",
   "(i) Diffusion (1). (ii) The beaker at 65 degrees C (1). (iii) Particles of matter are always in motion and there are spaces between them (1). (iv) At a higher temperature particles have more kinetic energy and move faster, so they mix more quickly (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> A student heats crushed ice from -10 degrees C steadily and records the temperature. It rises to 0 degrees C, stays at 0 degrees C until all the ice melts, then rises again to 100 degrees C, stays at 100 degrees C while the water boils, and rises further as steam is heated. "
   "(i) What is the melting point of ice? (ii) What is the boiling point of water at normal pressure? (iii) What is the temperature of the mixture while ice is melting? (iv) The temperature does not rise during melting even though heat is supplied. Explain.",
   "(i) 0 degrees C (1). (ii) 100 degrees C (1). (iii) 0 degrees C (1). (iv) The heat supplied is used to overcome the forces between particles and change the state from solid to liquid, not to raise the temperature (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> On a hot and windy day in Jaipur, Lakshmi's wet cloth dries in 30 minutes on an open terrace but takes much longer when spread folded in a closed room. Her grandmother also keeps water in a clay pot (matka) which keeps the water cool. "
   "(i) Name the process by which wet cloth dries. (ii) Name two factors that increase its rate. (iii) Why does the matka keep water cool? (iv) Why does spreading the cloth wide help it to dry faster?",
   "(i) Evaporation (1). (ii) Higher temperature, wind, larger surface area, lower humidity (any two) (1). (iii) Water seeps through the pores, evaporates from the outer surface and takes heat from the pot and water (1). (iv) A larger surface area lets more particles escape from the surface at the same time, speeding up evaporation (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

